That sort of depends on how you conceptualize
"reflection", it seems to me.

If a tiny amulet can fully reflect a beam, breath
weapon, or zap that presumably bathes the whole PC,
then the effect is "magical reflection", not
"physical reflection".

The same argument holds for the silver shield (of
Perseus).

Now you have to decide, which kind of reflection is
SDSM providing?

Since it _also_ doesn't cover the entire PC, but
protects as if it did, I'm guessing it's also
providing magical reflection, not physical
reflection, and so a cloak would not interfere.

My guess is that _all_ "reflection" in NetHack is
intended to be of the "magical" variety, and it
surely is implemented that way, since it all works
the same right now.

To change how it works, would, I'd guess, require
making two varieties of reflection in the source
code flags, magical and physical, and
differentiating them adequately.

Query: if "physical reflection" is to be
implemented, should crystal plate mail, plate mail,
and bronze plate mail, all fairly physically
reflective, confer 50% chance of reflection per
attack (or higher, but not 100%, and perhaps
a different percent for each, and dropping with
rust damage for the two rustable ones)?

That modest amount of earlier-in-the-game partial
reflection advantage, would be offset by the the
fairly huge disadvantage of wearing the extreme
weight of any of these three armors, so it would
be modestly game-balanced.

Quantum valeat.

